git fetch fetches updates but does not merge them.
The Git Fetch command is used to fetch all changes from the remote repository to the local repository.
It doesn’t make any changes to the current working directory. It stores all the changes in a separate
branch called the remote-tracking branch. git merge or git rebase command is used to merge these changes
into our current working directory.
Step 1:git pull == git fetch
step 2:git merge
git pull does a git fetch under the hood and then a merge.
git pull == git fetch + git merge

Start with Two Branches: Let's say you have two branches, main and feature.
feature is where you've been working on new changes, but in the meantime, others have added new commits to main.

Rebasing Process: When you run git rebase main from the feature branch, Git:

Temporarily saves all your feature branch commits.
Moves feature to the latest main.
"Replays" your saved feature commits on top of this updated main.
Result: Now, your feature branch has all the latest updates from main, plus your work on top of it, as if you had started
from the latest main the whole time. This results in a linear, cleaner history.

Why Use Git Rebase?
Clean History: Makes it easier to read through commit history without merge commits.
Easier to Follow: Rebase simplifies the branch history by avoiding extra merges.
Updates Your Work: Keeps your branch up-to-date with the latest changes on the main branch, which helps avoid conflicts later.
Example Commands

# Checkout to your feature branch
git checkout feature

# Rebase your branch on top of main
git rebase main
After this, your feature branch history will look as if it was based on the most recent commits from main.

A Note on Conflicts
If conflicts occur during the rebase, Git will pause and ask you to resolve them. After resolving, use:

git rebase --continue
Or, to stop the rebase if it’s too complicated:


git rebase --abort
In summary: git rebase rewrites your branch’s history to make it look like it started from the latest main branch version,
creating a tidy, linear history.